## w3c/trace-context — conformance test-suite cases

`src/w3c_vectors.zig`'s `vectors` hand-transcribes the request-header/verdict
data encoded in the W3C `trace-context` conformance suite's
`test/test.py` (classes `TraceContextTest`, `AdvancedTest`,
`TraceContext2Test`), published at https://github.com/w3c/trace-context,
pinned at commit `acab820be9db7b3433668baa5cdd43f57f4c4be0` (`main` branch,
2026-06-29):

  * transcribed by hand into `src/w3c_vectors.zig` (one entry per
    `self.make_single_request_and_get_tracecontext(...)`/`self.make_request(...)`
    call in the cited `source_test`, since the suite itself is a live
    two-way HTTP harness — Python `client.py`/`server.py` driving a running
    vendor service and inspecting its outgoing headers — with no separate
    JSON/CSV corpus file to `@embedFile`)
  * driven mechanically by `src/w3c_conformance_test.zig` (the runner, which
    replays each vector's headers through this module's own
    `TraceContext` middleware over the offline `http.Server.serveStream`
    wire harness already used by this module's hand-written tests, and
    checks the resulting `traceparent`/`tracestate` against the vector's
    verdict)

Excluded from vendoring, deliberately (each `out_of_scope` vector is still
counted by `w3c_conformance_test.zig`'s canary, never silently dropped — see
its header comment for the full reasoning):

  * forward-compatible parsing of `traceparent` versions other than `"00"`
    (upstream's `test_traceparent_version_0xcc`): the spec's "Versioning of
    traceparent" section uses SHOULD, not MUST, for this fallback parse, and
    this module deliberately does not implement it — recorded as a scope
    decision in `SPEC.md`'s Backlog, not a silent gap.
  * strict tracestate list-member GRAMMAR (illegal key/value characters, the
    256-char key cap, the 32-member cap): this module's tracestate
    validation is a deliberate light byte-class guard over the whole
    combined value, not a per-member parser (see `src/root.zig`'s
    `isValidState` doc). Every excluded vector here comes from an upstream
    test the suite itself gates behind `STRICT_LEVEL >= 2` — upstream
    already treats this as an opt-in stricter tier, not its Level-1
    baseline.
  * session/connection-repetition tests (`AdvancedTest`): properties of a
    stateful vendor HTTP service across several round trips, already
    covered by this module's own pre-existing id-generation tests.
  * Trace Context Level 2 (`TraceContext2Test`, the random-trace-id flag):
    explicitly out of this module's documented Level-1-only scope, and
    gated by the suite's own `SPEC_LEVEL >= 2` (off by default upstream).

  Also excluded entirely, not vendored as vectors at all: `test/tracecontext/
  test_traceparent.py`, `test_tracestate.py`, `traceparent.py`, `tracestate.py`
  — these test the SUITE'S OWN Python helper classes
  (`BaseTraceparent`/`Traceparent`/`Tracestate`), not wire-format conformance
  of a vendor implementation; there is nothing in them to transcribe as a
  request/verdict case.

